[cot:thought] I railroad employment trends. Peak employment was roughly 160???180,000 in the mid-2010s. Precision scheduling began around 2017???2018 and cut headcount to about 115???130,000 by 2022???2023. Terminal dwell hours rose during early implementation.
[cot:thought] The core question is whether service degradation preceded the headcount decline.
[cot:thought] The narrative arc
[cot:thought] is now clear. Crew cuts and longer trains caused initial service degradation. Terminal dwell rose and velocity fell at some carriers before stabilizing. Headcount kept falling even after the model settled.
[cot:thought] The headline will contrast Class I employment with dwell time. AAR figures show roughly 163,000 in 2015. That dropped to about 133,000 in 2019 and 115,000 in 2022. Composite data will reflect these known public trends.
[cot:thought] Sources include STB monthly employment reports and AAR rail performance data for terminal dwell hours, cars on line, and train speed. The data module will be written and committed as phase one.
